From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Hoban <bryan.hoban@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/i915/hdmi: Standard Dolby Vision RGB 8 bpc tunnel

PC players send TV-led Dolby Vision as a tunnel: the player packs 12-bit
YCbCr 4:2:2 and the per-frame metadata into an 8-bit RGB framebuffer,
and the display engine has to deliver those bytes to the TV unchanged,
with the InfoFrames that make the TV decode them.

Connector properties, on native HDMI connectors of display version 12
and later (i915.enable_dv_lab):

- DVBRIDGE_DV_STANDARD_LAB (bool): the tunnel is on. A change is a full
  modeset, never the fastset path; it reads 0 again once the connector
  has no CRTC.
- DVBRIDGE_TUNNEL_FORMAT (enum): what the sink is told the bytes are.
  The pipe and scanout are the same in every format.
  dolby-vision (0, the default): AVI RGB, no colorimetry, full range,
  the CTA VIC; the Dolby VSIF, or for v0 and long v1 sinks the HDMI-1.4b
  form with its HDMI VIC; no HDR metadata.
  hdr10: AVI YCbCr 4:2:2, BT.2020, limited range, the CTA VIC; no vendor
  InfoFrame; the HDR static metadata InfoFrame from HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA,
  which has to be there (type 1, PQ). The sink needs YCbCr 4:2:2 and PQ
  in its EDID, not a Dolby block.
  lldv: source-led Dolby Vision. AVI as hdr10; the Dolby VSIF with its
  low-latency bit; no HDR metadata. The sink needs the Dolby block's
  low-latency interface.
  Changing the format of an engaged tunnel is a full modeset.

Link policy (intel_dv_lab_policy.h, dvbridge_edid.h and
dvbridge_tunnel_format.h: MIT, inline, no hardware access, so amdgpu can
include them): a sink whose EDID has what the format needs (a bounded
parser for the Dolby vendor video block, v0/v1/v2; the userspace copy in
Kodi has to stay identical), native HDMI (a dual-mode level-shifter ID
on a native port is not a converter; an active LSPCON is), RGB 8 bpc
full range, max bpc 8, Colorspace Default, no cloning, progressive CTA
1080p/2160p at 24/25/30/50/60 Hz, 2160p above 30 Hz in the dolby-vision
format only if the Dolby block allows it, the sink's TMDS limit never
overridden, dithering off.

Scanout policy, in the CRTC's atomic check: no colour processing (CTM,
LUTs, CSC), no VRR, DSC or scaling, and the primary plane alone with an
unscaled, unrotated, opaque XRGB8888 buffer of the mode's size. A user's
LUT is refused, not silently bypassed. The HDR10 tunnel has no vendor
packet and is recognised by an RGB pipe with a YCbCr 4:2:2 AVI.

Rejections are logged with their cause, rate-limited: "DVBridge: link
rejected format=... errno=...", "DVBridge: scanout rejected ...
reasons=0x...", "DVBridge: HDMI format/clock rejected ...".

Also here, from the same original patch set: with PQ HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA
and max bpc >= 10 on Lunar Lake, an RGB link below 10 bpc is tried again
as YCbCr 4:2:0 and the commit fails if that does not reach 10 bpc
(intel_dvbridge_hdr_policy.h).

Passing these policies is not a Dolby Vision colour or fidelity
validation: pixel packing and metadata are the player's.

Origin: the Intel DV Linux transport of the native-dv Kodi port
(yblod-engine; the EDID parser's field layouts were checked against
libdisplay-info 0.4.0, see yblod-engine/docs/intel-dv/ORIGIN.md).
DVBRIDGE_TUNNEL_FORMAT is from big-fella (linux-yblod 7.3-9; research in
big-fella docs/research/hdr10-lldv-signalling.md: the enum's design,
Amlogic's LL packets).

Regrouped from linux-9900-00 to linux-9900-07,
linux-9901-native-hdmi-level-shifter, the i915 part of
linux-9902-hdmi-compatibility, linux-9903-diagnostics and the i915 half
of linux-9928-big-fella-tunnel-format (YBLOD 0.2 to 0.4.1); no code
change.
